      "text": "To solve the given system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nI will use the method of elimination. First, I'll eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of \\(y\\) opposites in the two equations. Let's eliminate \\(y\\).\n\nThe coefficients of \\(y\\) are \\(-5\\) and \\(2\\). The least common multiple of \\(-5\\) and \\(2\\) is \\(10\\). I'll multiply the first equation by \\(2\\) and the second equation by \\(5\\) to make the coefficients of \\(y\\) opposites:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n2(3x - 5y) & = 2(-11), \\\\\n5(7x + 2y) & = 5(-12).\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nNow, I get:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n6x - 10y = -22, \\\\\n35x + 10y = -60.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAdding these two equations eliminates \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60).\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), I get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41}.\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\(x\\) is:\n\\[\nx = -2.\n\\]\n\nNext, I'll substitute \\(x = -2\\) back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). I'll use the first equation:\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x = -2\\):\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nAdd 6 to both sides:\n\\[\n-5y = -5.\n\\]\n\nDivide by \\(-5\\):\n\\[\ny = 1.\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of \\(y\\) is:\n\\[\ny = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]",
