      "text": "To solve the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n3x - 5y &= -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y &= -12,\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\nwe can use the method of elimination. Let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, we will eliminate one of the variables. To do this, we need to make the coefficients of either \\(x\\) or \\(y\\) the same in magnitude but opposite in sign. Let's eliminate \\(y\\). The coefficients of \\(y\\) in the given equations are \\(-5\\) and \\(2\\). We can make these coefficients opposites by multiplying the first equation by \\(2\\) and the second equation by \\(5\\). This gives us:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n2(3x - 5y) &= 2(-11), \\\\\n5(7x + 2y) &= 5(-12).\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's simplify these equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n6x - 10y &= -22, \\\\\n35x + 10y &= -60.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add the two equations to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n41x = -82.\n\\]\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x = -2\\), we substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). Let's use the first equation:\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(x = -2\\), we get:\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11.\n\\]\nSimplifying, we have:\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\nAdding 6 to both sides, we get:\n\\[\n-5y = -5.\n\\]\nDividing both sides by \\(-5\\), we find:\n\\[\ny = 1.\n\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]",
